Islam is built upon five fundamental pillars that serve as the basis for a Muslim's faith and religious practices [[qa:icadb-book:389:21, qa:icadb-book:382:17]]. These pillars are established to create an integrated system that organizes various aspects of human life [[qa:icadb-book:389:21]].

{{hadith:hadeethenc:65000}}

{{hadith:hadeethenc:66512}}

The five pillars are as follows:
1. The testimony (Shahada) that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah and that Muhammad is His servant and messenger [[qa:icadb-book:382:17, qa:dorar-feqhia:674:1]]. This involves believing in the Oneness of God and submitting to His guidance [[tafsir:muyassar:42:13]].
2. Establishing prayer (Salah) [[qa:icadb-book:382:17, qa:dorar-feqhia:674:1]]. It is considered the most important pillar after the testimony [[qa:dorar-feqhia:674:1]].
3. Giving Zakat (almsgiving) [[qa:icadb-book:382:17, qa:dorar-feqhia:2094:1]]. This involves giving a small portion of one's savings to the poor and needy [[qa:icadb:36237]].
4. Fasting during the month of Ramadan [[qa:icadb-book:382:17, qa:dorar-feqhia:2094:1]]. This practice helps develop self-control and feelings of mercy [[qa:icadb:36237]].
5. Performing Hajj (pilgrimage) to the House (Kaaba) for those who are able to do so [[qa:icadb-book:382:17, qa:dorar-feqhia:2094:1]]. It serves as a symbol of unity in devotion to the Creator [[qa:icadb:36237]].

